e various dependent variables. (1 point)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"ridge_coeffs <- coef(cv_ridge, s = cv_ridge$lambda.min)\nprint(ridge_coeffs)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-15T14:59:15.336063Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-15T14:59:15.338124Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-15T14:59:15.360653Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-09-15T14:59:15.358850Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"11 x 1 sparse Matrix of class \"dgCMatrix\"\n                                        s1\n(Intercept)                  -0.0273086235\naverage_combat_score          0.0007804178\nkill_deaths                   0.3632606715\nkill_assists_survived_traded  0.2253596596\naverage_damage_per_round      0.0013777694\nkills_per_round               0.2316677881\nassists_per_round             0.1545582021\nfirst_kills_per_round        -0.1830767689\nfirst_deaths_per_round       -0.3841031403\nheadshot_percentage           0.0400169309\nclutch_success_percentage     0.0204566424\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":5},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**2)** Using your cross‑validated Ridge model (cv_ridge), calculate R² for both the training set and test set. Report RMSE and adjusted R² for test set. (2 points)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"X_test <- as.matrix(test_data %>% select(-rating))\nY_test <- test_data$rating\n\npred_train <- predict(cv_ridge, s = cv_ridge$lambda.min, newx = X_train)\npred_test <- predict(cv_ridge, s = cv_ridge$lambda.min, newx = X_test)\n\nsse_train <- sum((Y_train - pred_train)^2)\nsst_train <- sum((Y_train - mean(Y_train))^2)\nr2_train <- 1 - sse_train / sst_train\n\nsse_test <- sum((Y_test - pred_test)^2)\nsst_test <- sum((Y_test - mean(Y_test))^2)\nr2_test <- 1 - sse_test / sst_test\n\nrmse_test <- sqrt(mean((Y_test - pred_test)^2))\n\nn_test <- nrow(test_data)\nk_test <- ncol(X_test)\nadj_r2_test <- 1 - ((1 - r2_test) * (n_test - 1) / (n_test - k_test - 1))\n\ncat(\"Training R-squared:\", round(r2_train, 4), \"\\n\")\ncat(\"Test R-squared:\", round(r2_test, 4), \"\\n\")\ncat(\"Test RMSE:\", round(rmse_test, 4), \"\\n\")\ncat(\"Test Adjusted R-squared:\", round(adj_r2_test, 4), \"\\n\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-15T14:59:17.657645Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-15T14:59:17.659174Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-15T14:59:17.716444Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-09-15T14:59:17.714719Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Training R-squared: 0.9495 \nTest R-squared: 0.9422 \nTest RMSE: 0.0393 \nTest Adjusted R-squared: 0.9408 \n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":6},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The model performs exceptionally well. The very close values between the Training R² (0.9495) and Test R² (0.9422) also show that the model generalizes very well and is not overfitting.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **II.** Lasso Regression","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**1)** How many coefficients were exactly zero in the Lasso model? What does this suggest? Which were the top two in terms of weights?? (0.5 points)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"library(tidyverse)\nlibrary(glmnet)\n\ncv_lasso <- cv.glmnet(X_train, Y_train, alpha = 1)\noptimal_lambda_lasso <- cv_lasso$lambda.min\n\nlasso_coeffs <- coef(cv_lasso, s = optimal_lambda_lasso)\n\nzero_coeffs_count <- sum(lasso_coeffs == 0)\n\ncoeffs_df <- as.data.frame(as.matrix(lasso_coeffs))\ncoeffs_df$Variable <- rownames(coeffs_df)\ncolnames(coeffs_df)[1] <- \"Coefficient\"\n\ntop_two_coeffs <- coeffs_df %>%\n  filter(Variable != \"(Intercept)\") %>%\n  arrange(desc(abs(Coefficient))) %>%\n  head(2)\n\nprint(paste(\"Number of coefficients set to zero:\", zero_coeffs_count))\nprint(\"Top two predictors by coefficient weight:\")\nprint(top_two_coeffs[, c(\"Variable\", \"Coefficient\")])","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-15T14:59:20.604918Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-15T14:59:20.606530Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-15T14:59:20.744848Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-09-15T14:59:20.743119Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"[1] \"Number of coefficients set to zero: 1\"\n[1] \"Top two predictors by coefficient weight:\"\n                                     Variable Coefficient\nkill_deaths                       kill_deaths   0.5697049\nfirst_deaths_per_round first_deaths_per_round  -0.3516804\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":7},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**2)** Did Lasso outperform OLS and Ridge in terms of Test R² and RMSE? Why or why not?(answer based on VIF standings) (1 point)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":" library(car)  \n\n X_df <- as.data.frame(X_train)\n\n dummy_y <- rnorm(nrow(X_df))  \n\n lm_model <- lm(dummy_y ~ ., data = X_df)\n\n vif_values <- vif(lm_model)\n\n vif_df <- data.frame(\n   feature = names(vif_values),\n   VIF = as.numeric(vif_values)\n ) %>% arrange(desc(VIF))\n\nprint(vif_df)\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-15T14:59:24.117606Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-15T14:59:24.119319Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-15T14:59:24.253357Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-09-15T14:59:24.251663Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","text":"Loading required package: carData\n\n\nAttaching package: ‘car’\n\n\nThe following object is masked from ‘package:dplyr’:\n\n    recode\n\n\nThe following object is masked from ‘package:purrr’:\n\n    some\n\n\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stdout","text":"                        feature       VIF\n1          average_combat_score 52.112806\n2               kills_per_round 37.215079\n3      average_damage_per_round 23.229262\n4                   kill_deaths 10.248355\n5  kill_assists_survived_traded  3.667095\n6         first_kills_per_round  2.915132\n7             assists_per_round  2.268269\n8        first_deaths_per_round  2.056995\n9           headshot_percentage  1.176073\n10    clutch_success_percentage  1.055983\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":8},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Yes with these new VIF results, it is now highly likely that both Lasso and Ridge will significantly outperform a standard OLS model. Lasso may also have a slight edge over Ridge.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **III.** Inferences","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**1)** Suggest at least two additional features (not in the current dataset) that could improve player rating prediction","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Agent-Specific Ability Metrics, Team Performance and Match Outcome could be the 2 additional features.**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**2)** How does regularization help reduce overfitting in both Ridge and Lasso?","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Regularization reduces overfitting by adding a penalty for complexity to the model's loss function. This penalty discourages the model from fitting the noise in the training data by keeping the coefficient values smaller and the model simpler.**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### **IV.** Prediction of player ratings\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**1)** Using the model that performs better, predict the rating for the following hypothetical player  (0.5 points)\n* kills_per_round              : 0.78\n* average_damage_per_round     : 160\n* average_combat_score         : 245\n* kill_deaths                  : 1.30\n* assists_per_round            : 0.32\n* first_kills_per_round        : 0.18\n* first_deaths_per_round       : 0.14","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"library(tidyverse)\n\nhypothetical_player <- data.frame(\n  kills_per_round = 0.78,\n  average_damage_per_round = 160,\n  average_combat_score = 245,\n  kill_deaths = 1.30,\n  assists_per_round = 0.32,\n  first_kills_per_round = 0.18,\n  first_deaths_per_round = 0.14\n)\n\nmean_headshot <- mean(train_data$headshot_percentage)\nmean_clutch <- mean(train_data$clutch_success_percentage)\nmean_kast <- mean(train_data$kill_assists_survived_traded)\n\nhypothetical_player$headshot_percentage <- mean_headshot\nhypothetical_player$clutch_success_percentage <- mean_clutch\nhypothetical_player$kill_assists_survived_traded <- mean_kast\n\nplayer_reordered <- hypothetical_player[, colnames(X_train)]\n\ntrain_center <- attr(scale(X_train), \"scaled:center\")\ntrain_scale <- attr(scale(X_train), \"scaled:scale\")\n\nplayer_scaled <- as.matrix(sweep(sweep(player_reordered, 2, train_center, \"-\"), 2, train_scale, \"/\"))\n\npredicted_rating <- predict(cv_lasso, s = cv_lasso$lambda.min, newx = player_scaled)\n\nprint(paste(\"Predicted Rating:\", round(predicted_rating[1], 4)))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-15T15:06:01.735771Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-15T15:06:01.737496Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-15T15:06:01.791098Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-09-15T15:06:01.788809Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"[1] \"Predicted Rating: 0.7554\"\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":9},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**2)** Use the same model to get ratings for ten players at random from test dataset and compare the values.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"set.seed(123)\n\nrandom_players <- test_data %>%\n  slice_sample(n = 10)\n\nX_random <- as.matrix(random_players %>% select(-rating))\nY_random <- random_players$rating\n\npredicted_ratings <- predict(cv_lasso, s = cv_lasso$lambda.min, newx = X_random)\n\ncomparison_df <- data.frame(\n  Actual_Rating = Y_random,\n  Predicted_Rating = as.vector(predicted_ratings),\n  Difference = Y_random - as.vector(predicted_ratings)\n)\n\nprint(round(comparison_df, 4))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-15T15:16:08.799597Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-15T15:16:08.801365Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-15T15:16:08.841405Z","shell.execute_reply":"202